  },
  {
    key: 'schema-invariants',
    prompt: `Focus: SCHEMA INVARIANTS THAT CODE SILENTLY DEPENDS ON. The false positive existed because 'connections_chat_slug_unique' (a partial unique index, in db/migrations/20260630000020_connections_chat_slug_unique.sql at REPO ROOT) is the sole reason an org-less query in packages/server/src/lobu/stores/postgres-stores.ts (~line 361) is correct. Nothing at the call site says so.
Investigate: (a) How many other code paths depend on a non-obvious DB constraint for their correctness? grep for org-less/unscoped queries and cross-reference the index set. (b) A regression test asserting that index exists ALREADY exists at packages/server/src/gateway/__tests__/chat-instance-manager-config.test.ts:338 — find whether other load-bearing indexes have equivalent coverage, or whether that one is the exception. (c) Propose a mechanical guard: e.g. a schema-invariant test that enumerates load-bearing indexes from a manifest and asserts each exists, so dropping one fails CI. Is there a way to auto-detect which indexes are load-bearing?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'throwaway-probes',
    prompt: `Focus: THROWAWAY PROBE CORRECTNESS. Two ad-hoc verification scripts written during the session were themselves buggy: one keyed data by bare method name causing cross-namespace collisions (agents.list colliding with list_feeds), another used a greedy regex that matched across map boundaries and printed the same set three times under different labels. Both produced plausible-looking WRONG output used to reason about authorization.
Investigate: (a) Is there a pattern to when hand-written probes go wrong here — specifically, regex-parsing TypeScript source vs importing the real module? The session's successful probes IMPORTED the actual exports (via bunx tsx) while the failing ones regex-parsed source text. Verify that distinction holds. (b) Propose a rule + helper: when verifying anything about exported constants/maps, import them rather than parsing source. Check whether 'bunx tsx' importing from packages/server works reliably as a documented pattern, and what the exact incantation is (note absolute paths were required). (c) Should such probes be required to self-check (e.g. assert expected total counts) before their output is trusted?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'confidence-calibration',
    prompt: `Focus: CONFIDENCE CALIBRATION. The session reported "85 confidence" that a cross-tenant read was exploitable, when a single unfiltered query would have refuted it. AGENTS.md requires a confidence score with "the one-line basis for it (what you actually ran/verified vs. what you assumed)".
Investigate: (a) Read AGENTS.md's confidence-reporting rule and assess whether it is actually specific enough to have prevented this. (b) The failing pattern: confidence was assigned based on evidence GATHERED, not on evidence that would REFUTE. Propose a concrete, checkable reformulation — e.g. "before stating confidence above X on a defect claim, you must name the single cheapest observation that would disprove it, and state whether you ran it." (c) Test your proposed rule against 3 real claims from this session (the feeds drift = true positive, the slug finding = false positive, the sibling-divergence = false positive) and show it would have separated them. If it would NOT have separated them, say so and propose better.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'existing-rules-decay',
    prompt: `Focus: WHY EXISTING RULES DIDN'T FIRE. This is the meta-question. AGENTS.md is ~59 lines of dense hard-won rules; docs/GOTCHAS.md is ~119. Memory already contained 'feedback_absence_needs_origin_main_grep' and 'feedback_guards_must_assert_coverage' — both directly relevant, both violated anyway in the same session that wrote them.
